To use this you will need to have a working copy of GEOS.

***Running***
(1) Create a copy of userDefs_username.py where username is your LC name
    (for example userDefs_homel1.py) and point it to your GEOS executable.

(2) Create an input file named pfw_input_fileName.py, where fileName is the
    case name you want to run.

(3) Create a copy of runClean_username.sh, for example runClean_homel1.sh.
    Edit the fileNames list, fileLocation, and runLocation near the top of the
    script.  fileLocation may point outside the GEOS repository.  runLocation
    should be on a file system that can handle large files, such as Lustre or a
    workspace.

    The runClean script accepts either exact input filenames or case-name
    suffixes:

      fileNames=( "pfw_input_myCase.py" )
      fileNames=( "myCase" )

    The second form is interpreted as pfw_input_myCase.py.

Note: when using git, do not add your versions of userDefs_homel1.py or
runClean_homel1.sh to the repository.  These are specific to your file system.

(4) Run from the PFW directory:

      ./runClean_username.sh

    To run the particle file writer with multiple MPI tasks under Slurm, pass the
    number of tasks as the first argument:

      ./runClean_username.sh 8

You will see output as the GEOS input files and particle files are created.  If
you see an OOM error, you ran out of memory.

Change to the runDirectory to see the created files.

Type "squeue -u username" to see when the job will start.

***Input dependencies***
Input files can request additional files by adding comments of the form:

  #[pfw_dependency] path/to/file.dat

Dependency path rules:

  #[pfw_dependency] file.dat
      First looks for file.dat relative to fileLocation, then falls back to
      userDefs.pfwPath/file.dat for compatibility with older examples.

  #[pfw_dependency] tables/file.dat
      First looks for tables/file.dat relative to fileLocation, then falls back
      to userDefs.pfwPath/tables/file.dat.  By default the file is copied into
      the run directory using only its basename, file.dat.

  #[pfw_dependency] input:tables/file.dat
      Forces lookup relative to fileLocation.

  #[pfw_dependency] pfw:pfw_materials.py
      Forces lookup relative to userDefs.pfwPath.

  #[pfw_dependency] /full/path/to/file.dat
      Uses a real absolute path.  Legacy tags such as /pfw_materials.py still
      fall back to userDefs.pfwPath/pfw_materials.py if the absolute path does
      not exist.

  #[pfw_dependency] input:tables/file.dat => tables/file.dat
      Copies the dependency into a subdirectory of the run directory.  The
      destination must be relative to the run directory and cannot contain "..".

The runClean script writes .pfw_input_source_dir and exports
PFW_INPUT_SOURCE_DIR so particleFileWriter.py can resolve dependencies relative
to the original input-file directory after the input file has been staged into a
run directory.

Requested vector fields are written as native Silo vector variables.  For VisIt
Pseudocolor plots, scalar component expressions are also published under the
vector name, for example `gridVelocity/x`, `gridVelocity/y`, and
`gridVelocity/z`, so vector components appear in a grouped submenu instead of as
separate top-level scalar variables.

***Post processing***

Open VisIt and load the set of output_* files.

* You can add mesh plots, such as v_mesh for grid or p_mesh for particles, or
  add pseudocolor plots of particle data.
* Vector plots are possible but may be buggy.

***Packed spherical beds***

PFW includes `geom.packedSphericalBed`, a single geometry object for packed beds
of circular disks in plane strain (`dim=2`) or spherical grains in 3D (`dim=3`).
It is intended to be used instead of creating one `sphere` or `cylinder` object
per grain.

Minimal example:

  bed = geom.packedSphericalBed(
      "bed",
      x0=[pfw["xmin"], pfw["ymin"], pfw["zmin"]],
      x1=[pfw["xmax"], pfw["ymax"], pfw["zmax"]],
      dim=3,
      periodic=pfw["periodic"],
      materials=[
          {"mat": 0, "volumeFraction": 0.45,
           "meanDiameter": 1.0e-3, "stdDiameter": 0.10e-3},
          {"mat": 1, "volumeFraction": 0.15,
           "meanDiameter": 0.5e-3, "stdDiameter": 0.05e-3},
      ],
      overlapPercent=2.0,
      method="auto",
      seed=17,
      numContactColors=16,
  )

  pfw["objects"] = [bed]

The material volume fractions are absolute solid fractions of the box, so their
sum is the requested total bed fraction.  Particle sizes are diameters.  Positive
lognormal diameters are used by default when a nonzero standard deviation is
specified; use `distribution="normal"` for truncated positive normal samples.

`method="auto"` uses polydisperse RSA/cell-list insertion for lower fractions,
a lattice plus local relaxation for moderate dense 2D/3D cases, and an O(N)
triangular/FCC lattice path for dense or very large cases.  DEM import is a
placeholder; for now, users can pass `grains={"centers": ..., "radii": ...,
"mats": ...}` if centers and radii were generated externally.

For contact, the object returns a color-based `ContactGroup` instead of one group
per grain.  Keep `numContactColors` and any material offsets below the MPM
solver contact-group limit, commonly 100.

***Optional Silo grid-field output from pfw_input***

Silo output writes no extra background-grid fields by default.  To add selected
MPM grid scalar/vector fields to a generated Silo input, set one of these keys in
your pfw_input file:

  pfw["outputType"] = "silo"
  pfw["gridFieldNames"] = ["gridMass", "gridVelocity"]

The following aliases are equivalent and are accepted for convenience:

  pfw["siloGridFieldNames"] = "{ gridMass, gridVelocity }"
  pfw["siloGridFields"] = "gridMass, gridVelocity"

For a compact default debug set, use:

  pfw["siloGridFields"] = "common"

or:

  pfw["siloGridFields"] = True

The common preset expands to gridMass, gridVelocity, gridInternalForce, and
gridDamageGradient.  For a larger diagnostic dump, `pfw["siloGridFields"] =
"all"` expands to the MPM grid fields known to PFW, but this can substantially
increase plot-file size.

***Lagrangian tracer points***

The MPM solver can write one CSV history file per Lagrangian tracer.  Each tracer
is initialized from a user coordinate by selecting the nearest active particle;
after that, the tracer follows that particle ID.  Each output row is:

  t,x,y,z,<requested particle fields...>

A PFW input can specify tracer coordinates directly, or use the helper module
`pfw_tracerPoints.py` to generate grids and disks.

Example: a 10x10 square grid near the left end of a foam bar, normal to x:

  import pfw_tracerPoints as tracers

  dx = (pfw["xmax"] - pfw["xmin"]) / pfw["nI"]
  points = tracers.tensor_grid(
      x=pfw["xmin"] + 0.5*dx,
      y=tracers.linspace(-2.0e-3, 2.0e-3, 10),
      z=tracers.linspace(-2.0e-3, 2.0e-3, 10),
  )

  tracers.set_tracers(
      pfw,
      points,
      variables=["density", "velocityX", "plasticStrainMagnitude", "internalEnergy"],
      write_interval=pfw["endTime"] / 1000.0,
      output_prefix="leftEndTracer",
  )

Example: a disk of points on an x-normal surface to monitor velocity:

  points = tracers.disk(
      center=(pfw["xmax"] - 0.5*dx, 0.0, 0.0),
      radius=2.0e-3,
      normal_axis="x",
      radial_count=4,
      angular_count=24,
      include_center=True,
  )

  tracers.set_tracers(
      pfw,
      points,
      variables=["velocityX", "velocityY", "velocityZ", "speed"],
      cycle_interval=10,
      output_prefix="rightSurfaceVelocity",
  )

The generated solver attributes are `tracerHistory`, `tracerCoordinates`,
`tracerVariables`, `tracerWriteInterval` or `tracerCycleInterval`, and
`tracerOutputPrefix`.  Use a time interval or a cycle interval, not both.
